title Every priced column already answered, and a formula that predicted a different stub width
prompt Recur the autonomous loop -- Standfast on reds first, then take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, reading one council-rota row, and send it as its own signed round.
think Standfast first. crux/REDS.md holds rows %142, %143, and %144, and each carries a CLOSED companion line -- no open agent-closable red stands, so the line is not stopped and constructive work may open.
think Lap 184 by git log --grep "^caravan:" | wc -l, so the council rota reads row 184 mod 5 = 4, the Earth-Mercury triad: the one-clock naming law, TWO_ROOMS, and the marked value. TWO_ROOMS is the one that bore on this lap -- a claim earns the checkable room only once a witness binds it, which is exactly why every number in this round's prose had to stand asserted in the file printing it.
think The running thread named may_pass as the next head at 950 carried over fifty-one rungs, wearing both answerable columns. Read the harness before the meter: caravan/ladder_checks.rye imports system at its own head and already publishes waits(rung, table, at), so both priced columns were prices on an ordering rather than costs.
think Measured the cohort at the head of the lap rather than trusting the queue. Hashing the may_pass body across every rung that declares it found fifty-one identical; of those, eight keep a private Table -- confer, entrust, unhand below the fold line, and replace, revoke, taper, understudy, withdraw keeping the record type. That is the same eight the elder_waits lift left home, which makes it a property of the signature rather than a coincidence of one family.
think Read the scan's fall formula rather than guessing it: fall = (copies - 1) * lines - ((folding - 1) * stub + held_after), with held_after = (held - 1) * lines when held >= 2. At folding 43 and held 8 with lines 19 and stub 8 that returns 481, so the fall was arithmetic before the lift.
think Wrote the lifted body reaching waits(rung, table, i) in the harness and rung.items_contend for the contention rule, with rung.Table and rung.Reading in the signature. Replaced forty-three bodies with an eight-line stub each, keeping each rung's own visibility keyword.
obs Carry measured 63,445 -> 62,964 after the lift -- exactly 481. Two laps running the formula has predicted its own next reading, and this one did it across a different stub width (eight lines against three), so the prediction is a property of the formula rather than of one family's shape.
obs The first row of the arc to carry all three priced columns paid none of them. The function column answered from underneath (waits lifted a lap earlier), the import column answered from above (the harness imports system at its own head), and the record column answered by leaving five rungs home. A widening price is computed from where a name stands today, so it prices one ordering of the work rather than the work.
obs The eight rungs staying home are the same eight as last lap. Any family whose lifted signature names rung.Table meets exactly this set, which turns a per-lap measurement into a queue-ordering fact.
obs The reach witness's second row moved from choose_pass to mend_the_plan on purpose, because mend_the_plan is where the carry queue and the fall queue disagree most: 684 carried and only 72 of it recoverable, since a seventeen-line signature leaves a stub nearly as long as the body. Pinning the disagreement keeps a future lap from reading the carry queue as a fall queue.
obs The recited-count guard fired on this lap's own new fold-queue sentence and was right -- it named a family and a fall no assert in that file held once may_pass left the queue. The sentence was rewritten to point at the scan's own printed rows rather than the guard loosened.
obs choose_pass is the next head and the first row where the lift-underneath answer must reach two helpers rather than one, since its function column prices two openings per rung. That is a genuinely new question rather than another instance of the answered one.
